WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
N-grams.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.959
Single words lose local context.

3
00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:06.533
A sliding window brings some of it back.

4
00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:10.700
An n-gram is a contiguous run of n tokens.

5
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.933
One token is a unigram, two is a bigram, three is a trigram.

6
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:18.966
Slide a window across text and count what falls inside.

7
00:00:18.966 --> 00:00:20.867
That captures local context.

8
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.567
Here's the window in action on four tokens.

9
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:26.933
The first window grabs o2 and tank.

10
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.600
Slide one step, and we get tank pressure.

11
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.144
Slide again, and we get pressure low.

12
00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:34.267
Four tokens produced three bigrams.

13
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.333
Now count every window across a whole transcript.

14
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:42.037
Phrases like main bus and o2 tank rise to the top.

15
00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:45.100
These are units of meaning, not single words.

16
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.725
Trigrams go further.

17
00:00:46.725 --> 00:00:50.700
The page's example, main bus undervolt, is a precise phrase.

18
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.300
N-grams also hide a piece of history.

19
00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.211
N-gram language models predicted the next word from the previous few, using counts.

20
00:01:00.211 --> 00:01:01.833
That is GPT's objective.

21
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:05.367
The difference: a lookup table versus a neural network.

22
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.334
The table died of sparsity.

23
00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:11.967
Most five word sequences have never occurred, and neural models fixed that.

24
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:15.167
Here it is in a few lines of Python.

25
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.900
We lowercase the transcript and split it into word tokens.

26
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.567
Zipping the list with copies of itself shifted by one or two builds the windows, and Counter tallies them.

27
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.167
Then we just ask for the most common bigrams.

28
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.833
Here's a practical use.

29
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:36.780
Before designing chunking, you run bigram counts over a year of space-weather alerts.

30
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.367
Phrases like coronal mass and radio blackout dominate.

31
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.733
That tells you these phrases must never be split across chunk boundaries.

32
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:50.833
It also gives a phrase list so the keyword search matches coronal mass ejection as one unit.

33
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:52.917
Let's recap.

34
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:55.200
An n-gram is a run of n tokens.

35
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.200
A sliding window captures local context.

36
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.567
N-gram models predicted the next word by counting.

37
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:04.904
Sparsity is why neural models won.

38
00:02:04.904 --> 00:02:06.333
Next up: TF-IDF.
